body.translated_text:
Among these, the naive (<s>mugdhā</s>) —
The naive one is of tender age, newly amorous, contrary in love-making, gentle in anger;
she strives in the act of love with charming modesty concealed. ||1.151||
When her beloved has committed an offense, she gazes at him while weeping;
whether something unpleasant or pleasant, she does not say anything at all. ||1.152||

For example, in the <s>Anargha-rāghava</s> —
<s>Rāmabhadra</s> was led far away by the ruse of the golden deer;
then this youngest brother (<s>Lakṣmaṇa</s>) runs swiftly after him;
meanwhile, a mendicant enters the leaf-hut, trembling;
alas, alas, this <s>Rāvaṇa</s> reveals his own form! ||1.512|| (5.7)

From a new union, for example —
When her husband comes near the curtain, she lowers her face, born of modesty;
she desires a forceful embrace but withdraws her limbs imperceptibly;
unable to speak, she gives her eyes to her smiling-faced friend;
the new bride is inwardly tormented by shame at the first teasing. ||2.138|| (<s>Amaruśataka</s> 37)

From boldness (<s>prāgalbhya</s>), for example —
Sharing of one seat was avoided; meeting from afar was forgone;
even an eager embrace was thwarted under the pretext of bringing <s>tāmbūla</s> (betel);
nor was conversation mixed — keeping attendants busy nearby,
the clever woman, through courteous service to her beloved, made her anger bear fruit. ||2.148|| (<s>Amaruśataka</s> 18)

Fortunately, I did not leave because of anger born from hearing the half-told deception;
fortunately, nothing harsh was uttered by me when the story was half-told in anger;
fortunately, the tale came to its end, so as to reassure my bewildered heart;
fortunately, the world was not bereft of her who was falsely accused of infidelity. ||2.311|| (<s>Veṇīsaṃhāra</s> 2.13)

From causes residing in others, for example —
His limbs seemed to burst from the restraint of wailing by companions;
when the tears forcibly held back in his throat subsided, his voice became choked;
his mind races to embrace at the throat even those people praised in his inner thoughts.
Such is my condition — in the <s>Yadu</s> lineage, how can the rivulet of progeny survive? ||2.322||

Offense against a superior (<s>mantu</s>). From that, for example —
Seeing him approach with ornaments as offering in hand,
the lord of the people (<s>Rāma</s>) withdrew the <s>Sauparṇa</s> (eagle) weapon;
for the noble, when those [before them] are humble, do not persist in anger. ||2.334|| (<s>Raghuvaṃśa</s> 16.80)

From love toward animals (<s>tiryag-rāga</s>), for example —
The bee drank honey from a single flower-cup, following his beloved;
and the black deer scratched with his horn the doe whose eyes closed at the touch. ||2.528|| (<s>Kumārasambhava</s> 3.36)

For example, in the <s>Ratnāvalī</s>, the King (approaching, removing the noose) — 'O Queen! What is this improper act being done?
"My vital breaths are in my throat because of the noose around your throat;
this effort for the sake of a worthless goal — abandon this recklessness, O beloved!"' ||3.279|| [<s>Ratnāvalī</s> 3.16]

[Prakrit:] 'A widow fierce is the initiated wife of <s>dharma</s>;
liquor and meat are drunk and eaten;
alms are food, a scrap of hide the bed;
the <s>Kaula</s> doctrine — to whom does it not appear delightful?' ||3.497|| [<s>Karpūra-mañjarī</s> 1.23]
